2. Congestion Control Algorithm
AIM

To write a Java program to simulate the Leaky Bucket congestion control algorithm.

PROCEDURE

Start the program.

Read the bucket capacity and output rate.

Read the number of incoming packets.

Process each packet according to the bucket capacity.

Display the accepted and dropped packets.

Stop the program.

PROGRAM

import java.util.*;

public class CongestionControl {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter bucket capacity: ");
        int capacity = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter output rate: ");
        int rate = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter number of packets: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        int bucket = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            System.out.print("Enter packet size: ");
            int packet = sc.nextInt();

            if (packet <= capacity - bucket) {
                bucket += packet;
                System.out.println("Packet accepted");
            } else {
                System.out.println("Packet dropped");
            }

            int sent = Math.min(bucket, rate);
            bucket -= sent;

            System.out.println("Packets sent: " + sent);
            System.out.println("Packets remaining: " + bucket);
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT
Enter bucket capacity: 10
Enter output rate: 4
Enter number of packets: 3
Enter packet size: 6
Packet accepted
Packets sent: 4
Packets remaining: 2
Enter packet size: 5
Packet accepted
Packets sent: 4
Packets remaining: 3
Enter packet size: 8
Packet accepted
Packets sent: 4
Packets remaining: 7
3. Distance Vector Routing Algorithm
AIM

To write a Java program to simulate the Distance Vector Routing algorithm and find the shortest distance between network nodes.

PROCEDURE

Start the program.

Read the number of routers.

Read the cost matrix between routers.

Initialize the distance table.

Update distances using the Distance Vector algorithm.

Display the final routing table.

Stop the program.

PROGRAM

import java.util.*;

public class DistanceVector {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter number of routers: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        int[][] cost = new int[n][n];
        int[][] dist = new int[n][n];

        System.out.println("Enter cost matrix (999 for no link):");

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                cost[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
                dist[i][j] = cost[i][j];
            }
        }

        for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                    if (dist[i][k] != 999 &&
                        dist[k][j] != 999 &&
                        dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j]) {
                        dist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j];
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Final Distance Table:");
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.print("Router " + i + ": ");
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                System.out.print(dist[i][j] + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT
Enter number of routers: 3
Enter cost matrix (999 for no link):
0 2 7
2 0 1
7 1 0
Final Distance Table:
Router 0: 0 2 3
Router 1: 2 0 1
Router 2: 3 1 0
4. Link State Routing Algorithm
AIM

To write a Java program to simulate the Link State Routing algorithm using Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm.

PROCEDURE

Start the program.

Read the number of routers and cost matrix.

Select the source router.

Initialize distances to all routers.

Apply Dijkstra's algorithm to find shortest paths.

Display the shortest distance from the source to each router.

Stop the program.

PROGRAM

import java.util.*;

public class LinkState {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter number of routers: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        int[][] cost = new int[n][n];

        System.out.println("Enter cost matrix (999 for no link):");
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                cost[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
            }
        }

        System.out.print("Enter source router: ");
        int src = sc.nextInt();

        int[] dist = new int[n];
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

        Arrays.fill(dist, 999);
        dist[src] = 0;

        for (int count = 0; count < n; count++) {
            int u = -1;

            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if (!visited[i] &&
                    (u == -1 || dist[i] < dist[u])) {
                    u = i;
                }
            }

            if (u == -1 || dist[u] == 999)
                break;

            visited[u] = true;

            for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {
                if (!visited[v] && cost[u][v] != 999 &&
                    dist[u] + cost[u][v] < dist[v]) {
                    dist[v] = dist[u] + cost[u][v];
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Shortest distances from router " + src);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.println("Router " + i + ": " + dist[i]);
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT
Enter number of routers: 3
Enter cost matrix (999 for no link):
0 2 7
2 0 1
7 1 0
Enter source router: 0
Shortest distances from router 0
Router 0: 0
Router 1: 2
Router 2: 3
5. Error Detection and Correction Code (CRC)
AIM

To write a Java program to implement Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) for error detection in data communication.

PROCEDURE

Start the program.

Read the data bits and generator bits.

Append zeros to the data according to the generator length.

Perform modulo-2 division using XOR.

Obtain the CRC remainder.

Append the remainder to the original data to generate the transmitted codeword.

Stop the program.

PROGRAM

import java.util.*;

public class CRC {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter data bits: ");
        String data = sc.next();

        System.out.print("Enter generator bits: ");
        String gen = sc.next();

        int n = data.length();
        int m = gen.length();

        String msg = data + "0".repeat(m - 1);
        char[] bits = msg.toCharArray();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (bits[i] == '1') {
                for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                    bits[i + j] =
                        bits[i + j] == gen.charAt(j) ? '0' : '1';
                }
            }
        }

        String remainder = new String(bits).substring(n);
        String codeword = data + remainder;

        System.out.println("CRC Remainder: " + remainder);
        System.out.println("Transmitted Codeword: " + codeword);

        sc.close();
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT
Enter data bits: 1101
Enter generator bits: 1011
CRC Remainder: 001
Transmitted Codeword: 1101001

Note: This program calculates the CRC remainder and transmitted codeword. It does not separately verify a received message or correct corrupted bits. CRC is primarily an error-detection technique, not an error-correction code.

6. Socket Program for HTTP Webpage Upload and Download
AIM

To write a Java socket program to upload a webpage from a client to a server and download the webpage from the server to the client.

PROCEDURE

Start the server and listen on port 5000.

The client connects to the server using a socket.

The client sends a webpage upload or download request.

For upload, the client sends webpage content to the server.

For download, the server sends the stored webpage content to the client.

Display the transferred webpage content and close the connection.

PROGRAM

Use two Java files.

Server — WebServer.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class WebServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(5000);
        System.out.println("Web Server Started...");

        while (true) {
            Socket s = ss.accept();

            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(
                s.getOutputStream(), true);

            String request = in.readLine();

            if (request.equals("UPLOAD")) {
                String page = in.readLine();

                FileWriter fw = new FileWriter("page.html");
                fw.write(page);
                fw.close();

                out.println("Webpage uploaded successfully");
            } else if (request.equals("DOWNLOAD")) {
                BufferedReader file = new BufferedReader(
                    new FileReader("page.html"));

                String line;
                while ((line = file.readLine()) != null) {
                    out.println(line);
                }

                file.close();
                out.println("END");
            }

            s.close();
        }
    }
}
Client — WebClient.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.*;

public class WebClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        Socket s = new Socket("localhost", 5000);

        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
            new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(
            s.getOutputStream(), true);

        System.out.println("1. Upload webpage");
        System.out.println("2. Download webpage");
        System.out.print("Enter choice: ");
        int choice = sc.nextInt();
        sc.nextLine();

        if (choice == 1) {
            out.println("UPLOAD");
            System.out.print("Enter webpage HTML: ");
            out.println(sc.nextLine());

            System.out.println(in.readLine());
        } else if (choice == 2) {
            out.println("DOWNLOAD");

            String line;
            while ((line = in.readLine()) != null &&
                   !line.equals("END")) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        }

        s.close();
        sc.close();
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT

Server terminal:

Web Server Started...

Client terminal — upload:

1. Upload webpage
2. Download webpage
Enter choice: 1
Enter webpage HTML: <html><body>Hello World</body></html>
Webpage uploaded successfully

Client terminal — download:

1. Upload webpage
2. Download webpage
Enter choice: 2
<html><body>Hello World</body></html>

Important: This is a simplified webpage-transfer protocol built with Java sockets, not a standards-compliant HTTP server. It expects the HTML content on a single line and should be used only in a local practical demonstration.

7. Echo Client and Echo Server
AIM

To write and implement a Java socket program for communication between an Echo Client and Echo Server.

PROCEDURE

Start the Echo Server on port 5000.

Start the client and connect it to the server.

The client sends a message to the server.

The server receives and sends the same message back.

The client displays the echoed message.

Close the connection.

PROGRAM
Server — EchoServer.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class EchoServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(5000);
        System.out.println("Echo Server Started...");

        Socket s = ss.accept();

        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
            new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(
            s.getOutputStream(), true);

        String msg = in.readLine();
        System.out.println("Client: " + msg);

        out.println(msg);

        s.close();
        ss.close();
    }
}
Client — EchoClient.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.*;

public class EchoClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        Socket s = new Socket("localhost", 5000);

        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
            new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(
            s.getOutputStream(), true);

        System.out.print("Enter message: ");
        String msg = sc.nextLine();

        out.println(msg);

        System.out.println("Echo from server: " + in.readLine());

        s.close();
        sc.close();
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT

Server terminal:

Echo Server Started...
Client: Hello Server

Client terminal:

Enter message: Hello Server
Echo from server: Hello Server
8. Socket Program for Chat
AIM

To write and implement a Java socket program for chat communication between a client and a server.

PROCEDURE

Start the chat server on port 5000.

Start the client and establish a connection.

Allow the client and server to exchange messages.

Use separate threads to receive messages while sending messages.

Type bye to terminate the chat.

Close the socket connections.

PROGRAM
Server — ChatServer.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class ChatServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(5000);
        System.out.println("Waiting for client...");

        Socket s = ss.accept();
        System.out.println("Client connected");

        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
            new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(
            s.getOutputStream(), true);
        BufferedReader keyboard = new BufferedReader(
            new InputStreamReader(System.in));

        Thread receive = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                String msg;
                while ((msg = in.readLine()) != null) {
                    System.out.println("Client: " + msg);
                    if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("bye"))
                        break;
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.out.println("Connection closed");
            }
        });

        receive.start();

        String msg;
        while ((msg = keyboard.readLine()) != null) {
            out.println(msg);
            if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("bye"))
                break;
        }

        s.close();
        ss.close();
    }
}
Client — ChatClient.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class ChatClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Socket s = new Socket("localhost", 5000);
        System.out.println("Connected to server");

        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
            new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(
            s.getOutputStream(), true);
        BufferedReader keyboard = new BufferedReader(
            new InputStreamReader(System.in));

        Thread receive = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                String msg;
                while ((msg = in.readLine()) != null) {
                    System.out.println("Server: " + msg);
                    if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("bye"))
                        break;
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.out.println("Connection closed");
            }
        });

        receive.start();

        String msg;
        while ((msg = keyboard.readLine()) != null) {
            out.println(msg);
            if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("bye"))
                break;
        }

        s.close();
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT

Server terminal:

Waiting for client...
Client connected
Client: Hello
Hi, how are you?
Client: I am fine
bye

Client terminal:

Connected to server
Hello
Server: Hi, how are you?
I am fine
Server: bye

Note: Run the server first and then the client. Type messages into the appropriate terminal. The chat ends when either side types bye.

9. Socket Program for File Transfer
AIM

To write a Java socket program to transfer a file from a client to a server using TCP sockets.

PROCEDURE

Start the file server on port 5000.

The client connects to the server.

The client opens the file to be transferred.

The client sends the file bytes through the socket.

The server receives the bytes and saves them into a new file.

Display the transfer completion message and close the connection.

PROGRAM
Server — FileServer.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class FileServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(5000);
        System.out.println("File Server Started...");

        Socket s = ss.accept();

        InputStream in = s.getInputStream();
        FileOutputStream file = new FileOutputStream("received.txt");

        byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
        int bytes;

        while ((bytes = in.read(buffer)) != -1) {
            file.write(buffer, 0, bytes);
        }

        file.close();
        s.close();
        ss.close();

        System.out.println("File received successfully");
    }
}
Client — FileClient.java

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class FileClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Socket s = new Socket("localhost", 5000);

        FileInputStream file = new FileInputStream("send.txt");
        OutputStream out = s.getOutputStream();

        byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
        int bytes;

        while ((bytes = file.read(buffer)) != -1) {
            out.write(buffer, 0, bytes);
        }

        s.shutdownOutput();

        file.close();
        s.close();

        System.out.println("File sent successfully");
    }
}
SAMPLE OUTPUT

Server terminal:

File Server Started...
File received successfully

Client terminal:

File sent successfully
